WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.992
METR’s developer trials: the perception gap and the 2026 redesign.

2
00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:08.920
The July 2025 study (Becker and colleagues).

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:26.139
METR recruited 16 experienced open-source developers working on their own large, mature repositories, and randomized 246 real tasks to allow or forbid AI tools (mostly Cursor with Claude models of early 2025).

4
00:00:26.419 --> 00:00:29.896
Developers took about 19% longer with AI allowed.

5
00:00:30.176 --> 00:00:37.643
Beforehand they forecast a 24% speedup; afterward they still believed AI had sped them up by about 20%.

6
00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:47.715
Possible reasons the paper discusses include high familiarity with their codebases, high quality standards, and time spent reviewing and correcting AI output.

7
00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:50.427
The February 2026 update.

8
00:00:50.707 --> 00:01:00.989
METR ran a larger follow-up (57 developers, over 800 tasks) and reported that the data had become unreliable as a measure of current effects.

9
00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:20.853
Point estimates leaned toward speedups (about 18% less time per task for the 10 returning developers, with an interval from about 38% less to 9% more; about 4% less for the 47 new recruits, interval from 15% less to 9% more), but selection had crept in:

10
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:30.264
many developers would no longer work without AI, some withheld tasks they did not want to do without it, and a pay cut likely changed who participated.

11
00:01:30.544 --> 00:01:38.480
METR’s stated view: developers are probably sped up by current tools, the size is uncertain, and it is redesigning the study.

12
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.283
An upper bound from transcripts (17 February 2026).

13
00:01:43.563 --> 00:01:47.275
A separate METR note estimated time savings of roughly 1.

14
00:01:47.555 --> 00:01:57.901
5x to 13x on Claude Code-assisted tasks for seven METR staff, explicitly as a soft upper bound rather than an estimate of real productivity.

15
00:01:58.181 --> 00:02:02.811
The lesson is not “AI slows developers” or “AI speeds developers.

16
00:02:03.091 --> 00:02:16.936
” It is that measured effects depend heavily on population, task, and tool generation, that self-reports overstate, and that even a well-run trial can be undermined when the treatment becomes too useful for people to give up.
